---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../../components/Byline.astro";
import DualDoors from "../../components/DualDoors.astro";
import PillarLinks from "../../components/PillarLinks.astro";
import LegalNote from "../../components/LegalNote.astro";
import ThreeNumbersTable from "../../components/ThreeNumbersTable.astro";
import IndexSeriesTable from "../../components/IndexSeriesTable.astro";
import { BRIEFS } from "../../lib/briefs";
import { CANON_PATHS } from "../../lib/links";
import { newsArticleNode } from "../../lib/schema";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";

const brief = BRIEFS.find((b) => b.slug === "three-numbers")!;

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Notes", path: "/brief" },
  { name: brief.title, path: brief.path },
];

const pageUrl = "https://fflintel.com/brief/three-numbers";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${brief.title} | FFL Intel`}
  description={brief.dek}
  path={brief.path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.hero}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  extraJsonLd={[
    newsArticleNode({
      url: pageUrl,
      headline: brief.title,
      description: brief.dek,
      datePublished: brief.date,
      dateModified: brief.date,
      image: `https://fflintel.com/${CANON_PATHS.hero}`,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p
      class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-800 mb-3"
    >
      {brief.kicker}
    </p>
    <h1
      class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl text-balance mb-4"
    >
      {brief.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone-700 mb-6">{brief.dek}</p>
    <Byline dateLabel={brief.dateLabel} read={brief.read} />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-stone-200">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.hero)}
        alt={brief.imageAlt}
        width="1792"
        height="1008"
        class="w-full object-cover object-top max-h-80"
      />
    </figure>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        March: new guns sold at the same stores as last year were down 11.9
        percent, and 8.2 percent on the year. That is Gearfire’s Index, out
        April 13. Useful. It is last month. It cannot tell you what people
        will want next.
      </p>
      <p>
        One month is a blip. The whole year is the story. August 2025, the
        first monthly, was down 17 percent. November was the steep one: minus
        22.2. December put the 2025 year at minus 12.1. The annual later
        called it about 13 percent. January first came out at minus 6.2, then
        they moved it to minus 5.7 when more shops finished reporting.
        February: minus 6.5, year-to-date minus 6.1. March got worse.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800 mb-3"
      >
        The monthly numbers
      </p>
      <IndexSeriesTable />
      <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-stone-500">
        Last monthly we have is March. We will not invent April through July.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        They revise the number when more shops finish reporting. Their About
        says more than two thousand U.S. gun shops. It is still a sample. It
        is still last month. It is still only what sold.
      </p>
      <p>
        How they count: sales from the register and the shop website; new guns
        only; only stores that reported this year and last year. A shop that
        opened or closed does not pretend to be demand. They built it because
        a background check is a bad stand-in for a sale — permits, used guns,
        transfers, denials, two guns on one check. That part is right.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gearfire’s 2025 annual (NSSF, February 23): new-gun sales at the same
        stores down about 13 percent from 2024. Their 2026 guess: another
        seven to twelve percent down. What the monthly number will not tell
        you: shotguns −16 percent, rifles −15.4, handguns −11.5 (still the
        biggest category). Versus 2019, new guns −10.5 percent. Year-end
        inventory −10.6 percent. Ammo units −12.4. Optics down on the year;
        red dots more than doubled versus 2019. Suppressors −19.3 after a
        strong 2024 — they say some buyers waited on the $200 tax stamp.
        Suppressor inventory up 21.8 percent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Used guns can go the other way. Guns.com said local-dealer sales on
        their platform were up 46 percent in February, with used guns about 31
        percent of the mix. The Index is new guns only. Both can be true.
        Mixing them is how a forecast goes sideways.
      </p>
      <p>
        That sales number cannot see a search that came back empty. An empty
        search never becomes a unit sold. It still happened. We will not
        invent a Betsy percentage and park it next to −11.9 as if it were
        their Index. Different job. NICS is checks. RetailBI is what sold.
        Betsy BI is what people searched for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-800 mb-3"
      >
        Three numbers. Stop mixing these up.
      </p>
      <ThreeNumbersTable />
    </div>

    <DualDoors />
    <PillarLinks />

    <p class="mt-10">
      <LegalNote />
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
